    tags: ["languages"],
    body: "Biblical Hebrew is a Canaanite language, sibling to Phoenician and Moabite — the Israelites spoke the language family of their land. It was first written in the paleo-Hebrew script (seen on the Siloam inscription, Lachish letters, and early seals). After the Babylonian exile, Jews adopted the Aramaic 'square' script — the letter-forms in every Torah scroll and Hebrew Bible today. So the modern Hebrew letters are NOT the letters Moses or David would have written; the Samaritans still use a descendant of the old script. Hebrew faded as an everyday spoken language after c. 200 AD (remaining alive in prayer, study, and writing for 1,700 years) and was revived as a native spoken tongue in the 19th–20th centuries (Eliezer Ben-Yehuda's project) — the only fully successful revival of a non-vernacular language in recorded history.",
    deep: "Classifying Hebrew is not guesswork: it is done by comparing attested inscriptions. Hebrew, Phoenician, Moabite, Ammonite, and Edomite share so much vocabulary and grammar that linguists group them as Canaanite dialects within Northwest Semitic. The Mesha Stele, written in Moabite around 840 BC, can be read by anyone who reads Biblical Hebrew with only minor adjustments. Isaiah 19:18 itself calls the language 'the lip of Canaan' (שפת כנען), and 2 Kings 18:26 calls the Judean speech 'Yehudit'. The earliest inscriptions usually claimed as Hebrew — the Gezer calendar and the Khirbet Qeiyafa ostracon, both around the 10th century BC — are genuinely debated as to whether they are specifically Hebrew or a neighboring Canaanite dialect, which is why the date line above says 'attested from c. 10th century BC' rather than something more precise. By the 8th–6th centuries the evidence is unambiguous: the Siloam tunnel inscription from Hezekiah's Jerusalem (c. 700 BC), the Lachish and Arad ostraca from the last days of the kingdom of Judah, and hundreds of seals and bullae, all in the paleo-Hebrew script.\n\nThe script change is equally well documented and openly acknowledged in Jewish tradition. Paleo-Hebrew (ketav Ivri) is a national variant of the Phoenician alphabet. During and after the Babylonian exile, Jews living inside an Aramaic-speaking administration gradually adopted the Aramaic script (ketav Ashuri, 'Assyrian script'), whose letter-forms evolved into the square letters used in every printed Hebrew Bible and every Torah scroll today. The Babylonian Talmud (Sanhedrin 21b–22a) preserves rabbinic discussion attributing the change to the era of Ezra. The Dead Sea Scrolls catch the transition in the act: most scrolls are in the square script, a few (including some Leviticus material) are in paleo-Hebrew, and some square-script scrolls switch to paleo-Hebrew letters just for the divine name. Hasmonean coins and Bar Kokhba's coinage (132–135 AD) revived paleo-Hebrew as a nationalist gesture, and the Samaritan community still writes its Torah in a descendant of the old script — a living control sample.\n\nThe afterlife of Hebrew is the part most often told imprecisely, so the honest version matters. Hebrew did not die at the exile; Mishnaic Hebrew was still a spoken vernacular in parts of Judea into the 2nd century AD, and the Bar Kokhba letters include Hebrew alongside Aramaic and Greek. After roughly 200 AD it ceased to be anyone's cradle tongue, but it never became a dead language in the Latin sense: Jews prayed, wrote poetry, contracts, philosophy, and correspondence in it continuously. The 19th–20th century revival associated with Eliezer Ben-Yehuda — whose son Itamar Ben-Avi is usually counted the first native speaker of Modern Hebrew in modern times — turned that literary language back into a vernacular, now spoken natively by millions. Linguists do debate how continuous Modern Hebrew is with the ancient language (its pronunciation and some syntax show heavy European influence), but the revival itself, and its uniqueness on this scale, is not disputed.",
