  {
    title: "'Church': ekklēsia vs. the building",
    date: "translation history",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["definitions"],
    body: "The New Testament word is EKKLĒSIA — in ordinary Greek, an assembly of people called together; the same word describes a rioting secular crowd in Acts 19:32,39,41 and Israel in the wilderness in Acts 7:38. It never means a building in the NT (believers met in homes for the first centuries — documented archaeologically at Dura-Europos, the earliest known church building, a converted house c. 240 AD). English 'church' comes from a DIFFERENT Greek word, 'kyriakon' ('the Lord's [house]'), a building term. This mattered enough to die over: William Tyndale translated ekklēsia as 'congregation' rather than 'church' in his 1526 New Testament — one of the choices that enraged church authorities, since it relocated the word from the institution to the people. Tyndale was strangled and burned in 1536; the KJV (whose translators were instructed to keep 'church') nonetheless took the overwhelming majority of its NT wording from him.",
    deep: "Ekklēsia (ἐκκλησία) was a working political word centuries before Christianity. In classical Athens the ekklēsia was the citizen assembly — the body of those 'called out' (ek-kaleō) to deliberate — and the term stayed in ordinary civic use through the Hellenistic and Roman periods. The New Testament itself preserves this secular sense: in Acts 19 the word describes the riotous crowd in the Ephesian theater (verses 32 and 41) and, in the town clerk's mouth, the 'lawful assembly' where grievances belong (verse 39). The Septuagint had meanwhile used ekklēsia to translate Hebrew qahal, the assembly of Israel — which is why Stephen in Acts 7:38 can speak of 'the ekklēsia in the wilderness' at Sinai. When Jesus says 'I will build my ekklēsia' (Matthew 16:18 — one of only two occurrences in the gospels, both in Matthew), and when Paul writes to 'the ekklēsia of God which is at Corinth', the word denotes a gathered people, never a structure. The NT-era congregations met in private homes — Romans 16:5, Colossians 4:15, and Philemon 2 all greet a church 'in the house' of a named host — and the archaeology matches: the earliest identified church building, at Dura-Europos on the Euphrates (preserved because the city was abandoned after a siege in 256 AD), is a converted private house of c. 240 AD.\n\nEnglish 'church' descends from a different Greek word altogether: kyriakon (κυριακόν), 'belonging to the Lord', short for 'the Lord's house' — a term for the building, which passed into Germanic languages (Old English cirice, Scots kirk, German Kirche) probably via Gothic contact with Greek Christianity. So the English Bible tradition ended up translating a people-word with a building-institution word. William Tyndale, translating from the Greek in 1526, rendered ekklēsia as 'congregation' — and made parallel moves elsewhere: 'elder' rather than 'priest' for presbyteros, 'repent' rather than 'do penance' for metanoeō, 'love' for agapē. Thomas More attacked these renderings at length in his polemics against Tyndale precisely because they undercut institutional vocabulary; the dispute is documented from both sides in their published exchange. Tyndale, condemned as a heretic, was strangled and his body burned at Vilvoorde near Brussels in 1536. When the King James Version was commissioned in 1604, the translators worked under a set of official rules, one of which directed that 'the old ecclesiastical words' be kept — 'church' and not 'congregation' being the cited example. The KJV therefore prints 'church' throughout, even while modern statistical studies find the great bulk of its New Testament wording carried over from Tyndale.\n\nThe documented facts end there, and the reader's work begins. It is not a linguistic finding that institutional churches are illegitimate, nor that buildings are wrong — Christians needed and built them, and kyriakon is itself ancient. What the word history establishes is narrower and firmer: every NT statement about the ekklēsia is a statement about people, not real estate or hierarchy, and an English reader has to perform a small mental correction roughly a hundred times in the NT to hear the text the way its first hearers did. One caution keeps the balance honest: 'congregation' has its own drift problem in modern English (suggesting one local assembly), while ekklēsia in Paul can be local or broader — no single English word carries the Greek range perfectly, which is a normal, documented limit of translation rather than a scandal.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Acts 19:32, 39, 41 (ekklēsia as a secular assembly); Acts 7:38 (Israel in the wilderness)", url: "https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Acts+19%3A32-41%2C+Acts+7%3A38&version=NIV", where: "Bible Gateway; most modern versions render the riot 'assembly', showing translators recognize the ordinary sense." },
      { cite: "Dura-Europos house church (converted house, c. 240 AD)", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dura-Europos_church", where: "Wikipedia article with plans and photographs; the surviving frescoes are at the Yale University Art Gallery." },
      { cite: "Tyndale's 1526 NT and the KJV translators' instructions (rule 3: keep 'the old ecclesiastical words')", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyndale_Bible", where: "Wikipedia article on the Tyndale Bible and the article on the King James Version's composition, which reproduces the fifteen rules given to the translators." }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "'Baptize', 'Lucifer', 'virgin', 'cross': four loaded translations",
    date: "translation history",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["definitions"],
    body: "BAPTIZŌ: ordinary Greek for dip/immerse/plunge (used of sinking ships and dyeing cloth); the word was transliterated rather than translated into English — 'immerse' would have taken sides in a live church dispute. LUCIFER: Latin for 'light-bearer', Jerome's translation of 'helel' (shining one) in Isaiah 14:12 — a taunt aimed, in context, at the KING OF BABYLON (Isa 14:4). Its use as a proper name of Satan is later tradition (via patristic interpretation and Milton), not something the Hebrew text states; the same Latin word appears POSITIVELY of Christ in 2 Peter 1:19's Vulgate ('lucifer' = morning star). ALMAH (Isa 7:14): Hebrew for 'young woman (of marriageable age)'; the LXX chose 'parthenos' (virgin), which Matthew 1:23 quotes — the translation choice predates Christianity and the theological debate around it is exactly that, a debate. STAUROS: classical Greek 'stake/pole'; by the Roman period it covered the crucifixion apparatus generally; Roman crucifixion shapes varied (documented), the traditional cross shape is attested very early in Christian usage (the staurogram appears in NT papyri c. 200 AD; the Alexamenos graffito, c. 200, mocks a crucified figure on a cross shape). Each entry: the linguistic facts are documented; what to build on them is the reader's work.",
    deep: "Baptizō (βαπτίζω) is an intensive form of baptō, 'to dip'. The standard classical lexicon (Liddell-Scott-Jones) documents its secular range: to dip, plunge, or submerge — of ships sinking, of people drowning, of things soaked or, figuratively, of being 'soused' with wine or overwhelmed by debts. In the Septuagint it describes Naaman dipping himself seven times in the Jordan (2 Kings 5:14). The English Bible tradition, from Wycliffe's day forward, did not translate the word; it transliterated it, and 'baptize' became a church technical term with no everyday meaning. The effect is that English readers cannot hear whatever the original audiences heard, and the choice was not innocent by the 16th–17th centuries: rendering it 'immerse' or 'dip' would have taken a side in live disputes over the mode of baptism. The linguistic datum — ordinary word, meaning to dip or immerse — is documented; how far the physical image governs the rite's meaning and mode is a theological argument that the lexicon alone cannot settle, and honest writers on all sides acknowledge that NT usage also includes figurative senses (baptized 'in the Spirit', 'into Moses').\n\nLucifer is a Latin word, not a Hebrew one. Isaiah 14:12 taunts a fallen tyrant as helel ben shachar — 'shining one, son of dawn' — inside an oracle explicitly introduced, at Isaiah 14:4, as a taunt against the king of Babylon. The LXX rendered helel as heōsphoros, 'dawn-bringer'; Jerome's Vulgate used lucifer, 'light-bearer', the ordinary Latin name of the morning star (Venus). Early Christian interpreters, connecting the passage with Luke 10:18 ('I saw Satan fall like lightning'), read the taunt as a window onto Satan's fall, and that interpretive tradition — powerfully amplified by Milton's Paradise Lost — turned a Latin common noun into the devil's proper name in English. The KJV printed 'Lucifer' at Isaiah 14:12 and the name stuck. The documented ironies: the same Vulgate uses lucifer positively at 2 Peter 1:19 ('until the morning star rises in your hearts'), and Revelation 22:16 calls Jesus 'the bright morning star'. The Hebrew text of Isaiah 14 names Babylon's king, not Satan; whether a second, cosmic referent stands behind the taunt is an interpretive claim, defended and denied by serious readers — later tradition, not something the passage states.\n\nThe last two words carry the heaviest disputes, so precision matters most. Almah (עלמה) in Isaiah 7:14 denotes a young woman of marriageable age; Hebrew has a more specific word, betulah, usually rendered 'virgin', though even its usage has edge cases scholars argue over. The pre-Christian LXX chose parthenos — which normally means 'virgin', though its own usage is not perfectly uniform — and Matthew 1:23 quotes that Greek. Every link in that chain is documented; what Isaiah's sign meant in its 8th-century context, and how Matthew's use of it works, is genuine, centuries-old debate that a lexicon cannot close. Stauros (σταυρός) in classical Greek meant an upright stake or pale; in Roman-era usage it covered the crucifixion apparatus, whose configurations varied — ancient descriptions (such as Seneca's remark about crosses of different kinds) and the one recovered crucifixion victim's heel bone from Givat ha-Mivtar show variation is real. But the claim that Jesus' stauros was certainly a simple pole outruns the evidence in the other direction: the staurogram, a tau-rho monogram that visually suggests a cross with a figure, appears in New Testament papyri around 200 AD (P66 and P75), and the Alexamenos graffito, a pagan mockery scratched in Rome around the same era, depicts a crucified figure with arms spread on a crossbeam. The shape question is thus: variation documented, traditional cross-shape attested very early, certainty about the single instance unavailable.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Liddell-Scott-Jones, Greek-English Lexicon, entries baptizō and stauros", url: "https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0057", where: "Perseus Digital Library hosts the full LSJ; search the lexicon for βαπτίζω and σταυρός to read the entries with classical citations." },
      { cite: "Isaiah 14:4, 12 in Hebrew; LXX and Vulgate renderings; 2 Peter 1:19 (Vulgate 'lucifer')", url: "https://www.sefaria.org/Isaiah.14.12", where: "Sefaria for the Hebrew of Isaiah 14 with translation; Vulgate wording of both passages is printed in any edition of the Vulgate (e.g. the Stuttgart edition) and quoted in the Wikipedia article 'Lucifer'." },
      { cite: "Staurogram in early NT papyri (P66, P75); Alexamenos graffito (c. 200 AD)", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexamenos_graffito", where: "Wikipedia article on the graffito with image; the staurogram is treated in the Wikipedia 'Staurogram' article and in Larry Hurtado's published work on early Christian manuscripts." },
      { cite: "LXX Isaiah 7:14 (parthenos) quoted at Matthew 1:23", url: "https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Isaiah+7%3A14%2C+Matthew+1%3A23&version=NRSVUE", where: "Bible Gateway; the NRSV renders Isaiah 'young woman' and Matthew 'virgin', displaying the translation issue in one comparison." }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "How English itself drifted: KJV words that changed meaning",
    date: "1611 → today",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["languages", "definitions"],
    body: "Even a perfect 1611 translation misleads today, because English moved — every shift documented in the Oxford English Dictionary. 'Suffer the little children' meant ALLOW them; 'prevent' meant PRECEDE (1 Thess 4:15 — the living will not 'precede' the dead, not 'stop' them); 'let' could mean HINDER (2 Thess 2:7); 'conversation' meant CONDUCT/way of life (Phil 1:27); 'meat' meant FOOD generally; 'corn' meant GRAIN; 'peculiar people' meant God's OWN POSSESSION, not 'strange'; 'quick' meant LIVING ('the quick and the dead'); 'charity' translated agapē, LOVE. Reading a 400-year-old translation with today's definitions silently rewrites the text — one of the quietest, most common ways sincere readers end up believing things the original never said. The fix is documented and simple: check the original-language word and the 1611 sense, not the modern echo.",
    deep: "Semantic drift is not a theory about the KJV; it is a documented property of English, recorded sense by sense and date by date in the Oxford English Dictionary, whose historical method quotes actual usage from each century. The KJV's English was already slightly archaic in 1611 — the translators were revising a chain of versions reaching back through the Bishops' Bible and Geneva Bible to Tyndale in the 1520s–30s, and they deliberately kept much older wording. Four centuries later, dozens of its everyday words have shifted sense while remaining in use, which is more dangerous than obsolete words: a reader who meets 'astonied' knows to look it up, but a reader who meets 'prevent' thinks he already knows it. Linguists call these false friends across time.\n\nThe examples in the body can each be checked in seconds against a modern translation, and the differences are not trivial. In 1 Thessalonians 4:15 the KJV says the living 'shall not prevent them which are asleep' — from Latin praevenire, 'to come before'; the Greek (phthanō) means precede, as modern versions render it; read with the modern sense, the verse turns into nonsense about stopping the dead. In 2 Thessalonians 2:7 'he who now letteth will let' uses let in its old sense of hinder (a sense surviving only in fossils like the tennis 'let' and the legal phrase 'without let or hindrance'), the exact opposite of modern 'allow'. 'Suffer the little children to come unto me' (Mark 10:14) means allow them. 'Conversation' in Philippians 1:27 and 1 Peter 3:1–2 translates words for conduct or way of life — a wife's silent 'conversation' is her behavior, not her talk. 'Meat' meant food generally (so the 'meat offering' of Leviticus is a grain offering); 'corn' meant grain of any kind, not maize, which Europeans had barely met; 'peculiar people' (Titus 2:14, 1 Peter 2:9) renders a term for a treasured personal possession, not oddness; 'the quick and the dead' (Acts 10:42, 2 Timothy 4:1) means the living and the dead; 'charity' throughout 1 Corinthians 13 is agapē, love, narrowed in modern English to almsgiving; and 'study to shew thyself approved' (2 Timothy 2:15) means be diligent, not hit the books. 'Ghost' in 'Holy Ghost' and 'gave up the ghost' simply meant spirit.\n\nTwo honest cautions complete the picture. First, this is not a defect of the KJV: its translators did their work well in the English of their day, and the drift would have overtaken any translation — which is the standing argument for why translation has to be redone each era, and why the KJV's own preface (the Translators to the Reader) defends making new translations at all. Second, the scale should not be exaggerated: most KJV sentences remain clear, and its phrasing shaped English itself — David Crystal's study Begat traced hundreds of common English idioms to the KJV and its predecessors. The documented problem is narrower and sneakier: a specific set of shifted words where a modern reader silently substitutes a modern sense and comes away sure the text says something it never said. The remedy is mechanical, not mystical — compare a modern translation, or check the OED and the original-language word — and everything needed to do it is publicly available.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Oxford English Dictionary, historical entries for prevent, let, conversation, suffer, peculiar, quick, charity, meat, corn", url: null, where: "The OED (print second edition or the subscription online edition, available through most public and university libraries) documents each sense with dated quotations." },
      { cite: "KJV (1611) usage vs. modern translations, verse-by-verse", url: "https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Thessalonians+4%3A15&version=KJV%3BNIV", where: "Bible Gateway parallel display; repeat with 2 Thessalonians 2:7, Mark 10:14, Philippians 1:27, and 1 Corinthians 13 to see each shift directly." },
      { cite: "David Crystal, Begat: The King James Bible and the English Language (Oxford University Press, 2010)", url: null, where: "In print and in most large public libraries; traces KJV-derived idioms through English usage." }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "How we got the English Bible — and who paid for it",
    date: "1382–1611",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["languages"],
    body: "For roughly a thousand years the Western church's Bible was Jerome's Latin Vulgate, unreadable to the common person; translation into common tongues was suppressed (after the Wycliffite translations of the 1380s, the 1408 Constitutions of Oxford made unlicensed English translation punishable as heresy — people were burned with Wycliffite Bibles around their necks, and Wycliffe's own bones were dug up and burned in 1428, all documented). William Tyndale, working from the original Hebrew and Greek (not the Latin), printed his English NT in 1526; hunted across Europe for a decade, he was betrayed, strangled, and burned in 1536 — his last recorded words, 'Lord, open the King of England's eyes.' Within three years, England licensed an English Bible; the 1611 King James Version drew (by modern statistical estimates) 75–90% of its NT wording from Tyndale.",
    deep: "Jerome produced the Latin Vulgate around 400 AD precisely so ordinary Latin-speakers could read scripture — vulgata means 'common'. The irony of the next millennium is that Latin died as a common tongue while the church kept the Bible in it, so the 'common version' became a clerical preserve. Partial Old English renderings existed (psalters, gospel glosses, Aelfric's paraphrases), but the first complete English Bibles were produced by the circle of John Wycliffe in the 1380s, translated from the Latin and copied by hand. The reaction is a matter of record: the Constitutions issued under Archbishop Thomas Arundel (drafted at Oxford in 1407 and promulgated in 1409, conventionally cited as the 1408 Constitutions) forbade translating scripture into English or reading such translations without diocesan approval, on pain of heresy proceedings — and heresy, after the 1401 statute De heretico comburendo, could carry burning. Lollards were in fact burned in the following decades, trial records survive, and possession of English scripture repeatedly appears in the charges. The Council of Constance condemned Wycliffe posthumously, and in 1428 his bones were exhumed, burned, and thrown into the River Swift — a documented event, whatever one makes of its symbolism. Despite suppression, well over two hundred manuscript copies of Wycliffite Bibles survive, itself evidence of demand.\n\nWilliam Tyndale changed the terms of the enterprise: not English from Latin, but English from the original Hebrew and Greek, multiplied by the printing press. Refused permission in London, he worked in exile; his New Testament was printed in 1526 (an attempted earlier printing at Cologne was interrupted), and copies smuggled into England were bought up and burned by the authorities. He revised the NT in 1534 and translated roughly half the Old Testament, including the Pentateuch, coining or fixing in place phrases English still uses — 'let there be light', 'the powers that be', 'salt of the earth', 'scapegoat', 'Passover'. Betrayed at Antwerp by Henry Phillips in 1535, he was imprisoned at Vilvoorde castle near Brussels and executed in 1536 — strangled at the stake, his body then burned. Foxe's Acts and Monuments records his last words as 'Lord, open the King of England's eyes'; Foxe is a partisan source and details of his martyr accounts vary in reliability, but the arrest, trial, and execution are corroborated by surviving correspondence, including letters around Thomas Cromwell's attempted intervention. The eyes did open with startling speed: Coverdale's complete English Bible appeared in 1535, the licensed 'Matthew Bible' (largely Tyndale under a pseudonym) in 1537, and the Great Bible — ordered set up in every parish church — in 1539.\n\nThe 1611 King James Version stands at the end of this chain, not apart from it. Its translators were instructed to revise the Bishops' Bible against the originals, consulting Tyndale, Coverdale, Matthew, the Great Bible, and Geneva — and the outcome kept Tyndale's voice. The commonly cited modern computation (Jon Nielson and Royal Skousen, in the journal Reformation, 1998) found on sampled passages that about 83% of the KJV New Testament wording, and roughly three-quarters of its Old Testament wording in the books Tyndale translated, derives from Tyndale; hence the honest range of 75–90% given in the body. All of this is documented history rather than confessional claim, and its point for this project is simple: the accessibility of the English Bible was not inevitable or free, and the record of what it cost is part of reading it truthfully.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Constitutions of Oxford (Arundel's constitutions, 1407–09, cited as 1408)", url: null, where: "Latin text in David Wilkins, Concilia Magnae Britanniae et Hiberniae, vol. 3; discussed with translation in standard histories of the Wycliffite Bible and in the Cambridge History of the Bible." },
      { cite: "John Foxe, Acts and Monuments ('Foxe's Book of Martyrs'), accounts of Lollard prosecutions and Tyndale's death", url: "https://www.ccel.org/ccel/foxe", where: "Christian Classics Ethereal Library, author page for John Foxe; abridged editions widely available, full critical text via the Acts and Monuments online scholarly edition maintained by British universities." },
      { cite: "Jon Nielson and Royal Skousen, 'How Much of the King James Bible Is William Tyndale's?' Reformation 3 (1998)", url: null, where: "Journal Reformation, vol. 3 (1998); accessible through academic databases (e.g. via university library subscriptions) — source of the c. 83% NT figure." },
      { cite: "Tyndale Bible: printing history, smuggling, betrayal, and execution (1526–1536)", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Tyndale", where: "Wikipedia biography with bibliography; the standard scholarly life is David Daniell's biography of Tyndale (Yale University Press)." }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: "Kreyòl Ayisyen: a case study in language as survival",
    date: "formed 17th–18th century",
    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["languages"],
    body: "Haitian Creole belongs in any honest history of language, and it connects this section to Roots & Recovery. It formed within about a century on Saint-Domingue's plantations, where speakers of Fon, Ewe, Yoruba, Kikongo, and other languages, forbidden to be schooled in French, built a full language: French-derived words carried on West African (especially Gbe) grammatical structure — documented in detail by modern linguistics. It demonstrates two truths this whole section teaches: languages are not corrupted or pure, they are ADAPTED by people to survive their circumstances; and a people's grammar can preserve their ancestry even when every name and record was taken. Kreyòl gained co-official status only in 1987 and full standardized orthography in 1979–80 — the recovery of a suppressed inheritance, still in progress.",
    deep: "The historical setting is documented in colonial records: French Saint-Domingue became, across the 18th century, the most profitable slave colony in the Atlantic world, importing captives on such a scale — hundreds of thousands of embarked Africans are individually traceable in the transatlantic slave trade databases — that the enslaved population vastly outnumbered Europeans and was constantly replenished from Africa. People speaking Gbe languages (Fon, Ewe), Yoruba, Kikongo, and many others were thrown together with no common tongue and systematically denied French schooling. Within roughly a century a full natural language existed: French supplied most of the vocabulary (the lexifier), while the grammar came out strikingly different from French and strikingly parallel, in many features, to West African languages. The earliest surviving texts in recognizable Creole date from the late colonial period, so the language's formation is bracketed by hard dates even though its internal stages were never written down.\n\nWhat linguists have documented about its structure is the payload of this entry. Haitian Creole marks tense, mood, and aspect not with verb endings but with free preverbal particles — te for past, ap for progressive/future, pral for near future, ta for conditional — stackable in fixed order, a system with close analogues in Gbe languages and essentially none in French. Its definite article follows the noun (liv la, 'the book'), its pronouns do double duty as possessives, and it uses verb serialization (chains like 'take knife cut bread') familiar from West African syntax. None of this is 'broken French'; it is a different grammar wearing French-derived words. How exactly that grammar arose is, honestly, the genuinely debated part: Claire Lefebvre's relexification hypothesis argues the founders essentially kept Fon grammar and swapped in French word-shapes, while other creolists explain the outcome through second-language acquisition dynamics, feature competition among many input languages, or ordinary contact-driven change; Michel DeGraff of MIT has further argued, influentially, against 'creole exceptionalism' — the idea that creoles are structurally simpler or lesser languages — insisting Kreyòl is a natural language like any other, full stop. The substrate influence itself is broadly accepted; the mechanism and its degree are the live scholarly argument, and this entry's DOCUMENTED grade attaches to the language's history and structure, not to any one genesis theory.\n\nThe political history of the language is likewise a matter of record, and it is the reason this entry sits beside Roots & Recovery. For most of Haiti's existence virtually the entire population spoke Kreyòl while schooling, courts, and administration ran in French — a language most Haitians did not speak — making language itself a mechanism of exclusion. The standardized official orthography was adopted by decree in 1979 and finalized in 1980; the 1987 Constitution's Article 5 declares that all Haitians are united by a common language, Kreyòl, and makes Kreyòl and French the two official languages. Bible translation belongs to this story too: complete Kreyòl Bibles appeared only in the 20th century, meaning the majority language of the first Black republic waited longer for the scriptures than English did after Tyndale. The two lessons the body draws are fair conclusions from documented material: languages adapt rather than corrupt, and grammar can carry ancestry when names, records, and kinship lines were deliberately destroyed — with the caveat that grammar identifies contributing regions and language families, not individual family trees.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Claire Lefebvre, Creole Genesis and the Acquisition of Grammar: The Case of Haitian Creole (Cambridge University Press, 1998)", url: null, where: "The book-length statement of the relexification hypothesis with the Fon-Haitian comparisons; in university libraries." },
      { cite: "Michel DeGraff (MIT), work on Kreyòl's full linguistic status and against 'creole exceptionalism'", url: null, where: "DeGraff's papers in journals such as Language and in edited volumes on creole linguistics; accessible via academic databases and his MIT linguistics faculty publication list." },
      { cite: "Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade Database (scale and origins of the captive population of Saint-Domingue)", url: "https://www.slavevoyages.org", where: "SlaveVoyages database; filter voyages by principal place of landing to see Saint-Domingue's documented volume and African regions of embarkation." },
      { cite: "1979 orthography decree; 1987 Constitution of Haiti, Article 5 (Kreyòl co-official)", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haitian_Creole", where: "Wikipedia article's sections on orthography and official status, with citations to the decree and constitutional text." }
    ]
  }
